Usages:
  conventions: .goga/usages/conventions.md
  pluginator: .goga/usages/cooks/pluginator.md
  jinja2: .goga/usages/cooks/jinja2.md
  testcontainers: .goga/usages/cooks/testcontainers.md
  prettyplay: .goga/usages/cooks/prettyplay.md
  allure-pytest: .goga/usages/cooks/allure-pytest.md

Annotations: |
  Use `conventions` for code writing rules, relative imports, docstrings, and testing.
  Use `pluginator` for the plugin framework contract — @define.plugin, define.option, the option resolution chain, fixtures as plugin-class methods, install_pytest_plugins, call_context, and the configure() lifecycle hook.
  Use `jinja2` for the Jinja2 base_url engine: the Environment/StrictUndefined configuration, the custom match_re test, and the required-dependency contract.
  Use `api_usage` for consuming the `Api` facade and generated fixtures from tests.
  Use `prettyplay` for the web runtime contract the web fixture constructs — the PrettyConfig field table,
  PrettyPlay construction (cache_key/cache_path/config), and the StepHooks event set.
  Use `allure-pytest` for the guarded allure import and the StepHooks-to-Allure mapping with failure
  screenshots.

  pybuggy pytest-plugins cell. Provides the api fixture (function-scope) that constructs `Api` from plugin options, the sandbox and web plugins, and wires the plugins into pytest through `install`. A consumer enables them by calling goga_tool_pybuggy.plugins.install() from conftest.py — there is no import-time auto-wiring, so pytest_plugins = ['goga_tool_pybuggy.plugins'] alone does NOT enable the plugins. The generated endpoint fixtures resolve api into a working `Api`.

  base_url is a Jinja2 template string rendered once in the pluginator configure lifecycle hook (pytest configphase, before any test or fixture runs) against the rendering context = the full environment plus the CLI options the user actually passed (auto-captured from the CLI; see `pluginator`). The api fixture consumes the rendered value; there is no rendering inside the fixture. Rendering uses a single engine, Jinja2 (a required dependency), with StrictUndefined and a custom match_re test, so an unknown variable raises (URLs must not be silently truncated). The rendered value is stored back onto the base_url option for the api fixture to read. A plain URL without Jinja placeholders renders to itself, and every whitespace run in the rendered value is removed so a multi-line template (YAML folded (>) / literal (|) scalars, or a template with an empty Jinja block) yields one clean URL.

  The CLI flag for base_url is --base-url, and a typed --base-url is authoritative: configure applies its value with top precedence over the plugin config and the BASE_URL env before rendering (the pluginator chain alone resolves the config before the CLI; see `pluginator`). The CLI value is itself a Jinja2 template rendered against the same context.

  The plugins facade (__all__) re-exports PluginConfigKeys and WebConfigKeys as importable contract types for data-driven consumption by consumers, and re-exports all three plugin classes (ApiPlugin, SandboxPlugin, WebPlugin).

  Constraints:
  - `install` is NOT called at import time; a consumer must call goga_tool_pybuggy.plugins.install() from conftest.py. call_context() then resolves to the conftest namespace through the one-level wrapper, so the hooks and pytest_plugins land there.
  - `ApiPlugin` overrides BasePlugin.__init__ to accept context (and optional loaders) and to register discovered plugins synchronously.
  - `install` defaults loaders to [PackageLoader('api', required=False)] when omitted, so the generated api/ fixture tree is discovered out of the box; override with install(loaders=[...]), the loader config section (PluginConfigKeys.LOADER), or disable with install(loaders=[]).
  - Minimal fixture profile: base_url, headers, timeout, plus the assert-polling / pluggable-class options (assert_timeout/assert_delay/assert_field_class/assert_response_class) — no auth, no cookies.
  - The api fixture is a generator (yield + teardown): after the test it calls api.close() to close the Api (delegating to the underlying resq.Session's public close()).
  - base_url template rendering is eager: it runs once in configure at pytest configphase, so a missing required base_url surfaces there (not on fixture invocation). The api fixture reads self.base_url, which configure overwrote with the rendered value (configure always runs before any fixture invocation).
  - CLI placeholders in the base_url template require the consumer to register the matching options (e.g. --env) via pytest_addoption in conftest — pytest rejects unregistered options. Only options the user actually typed enter the rendering context; the full resolved option namespace is intentionally NOT used, to avoid leaking internal/plugin options.
  - Rendering is single-engine: every base_url template is rendered with Jinja2 (a required dependency); a plain URL without Jinja placeholders renders to itself.
  - Jinja2 uses StrictUndefined: an unknown variable raises — URLs must not be silently truncated.
  - The rendered base_url is URL-normalized: every whitespace run is removed (a URL never legitimately contains literal whitespace — it would be percent-encoded to %20 and break the request path), so YAML folded (>) / literal (|) multi-line templates and empty Jinja blocks do not leak whitespace into the URL.
  - A custom match_re test (a regex match anchored at start, taking the pattern as its argument) is registered for conditional URL assembly; Jinja2 has no built-in regex test.
  - Use relative imports inside the cell.

  The cell carries the fixture-driven sandbox activation: the installation entry gates on the
  presence of the sandbox configuration document — with the document the `SandboxPlugin` joins
  the same installation call and owns the product sandbox fixtures (a lazy session start, a
  per-test product reset, and the marked-preset enqueue). Binding an api address to the
  sandbox is explicit: the test body calls bind on the api client or an endpoint
  (see `api_usage`); the plugin cell owns only fixture construction and teardown.
  Use `sandbox-session` for the consumer fixture patterns of the sandbox.

  The cell carries the fixture-driven web activation: the installation entry gates on the presence of
  the web config document (the plugins-cell default path — the platform tool-config path named by the
  FILENAME member of the cell's own `WebConfigKeys`) — with the document the `WebPlugin`
  joins the same installation call and owns the web test kind: the web fixture (a per-test
  prettyplay runtime with the step cache next to the test file), the --strict-mode, --interactive,
  and --browser-endpoint options, Allure step reporting, and the prettyplay event log formatter.
  The web config key set (the canonical key set of web.yml) and the document validation live in
  this cell — the web plugin reads and
  validates its own document in its configure step — an invalid document fails the run at
  configphase; without the document nothing web-related is registered, and a test requesting the web
  fixture gets pytest's standard "fixture 'web' not found".
  Use `pybuggy-web` from Imports for the consumer patterns of web tests.

---

"ApiPlugin(*, context: dict, loaders: list[BaseLoader] | None = None, default_retries: int | None = None, default_assert_timeout: int | None = None, default_assert_delay: int | float | None = None)":
  location: api_plugin.py
  annotations: |
    Plugin class providing configurable options and the api fixture. Decorated with @define.plugin under the name pybuggy-api (the pytest option-group and pluginmanager registration name), bound to the pybuggy api config file.

    `context`: the namespace dict the plugin installs its hooks into; its pytest_plugins key is populated on construction.
    `loaders`: explicit loaders in addition to those in the loader config section; defaults to none.
    `default_retries`: default flaky rerun count for the retries option when neither the config key nor the --retries CLI is set; defaults to none (the option then resolves to 0 / no reruns).
    `default_assert_timeout`: default baseline assert-polling timeout for the assert_timeout option when neither the config key nor the --api-assert-timeout CLI is set; defaults to none (no polling unless configured).
    `default_assert_delay`: default baseline assert-polling delay for the assert_delay option when neither the config key nor the --api-assert-delay CLI is set; defaults to none.

    Algorithm:
    1. Call super().__init__() so BasePlugin resolves the config file.
    2. Store default_retries/default_assert_timeout/default_assert_delay as the default_from sources for their options.
    3. Assemble the recursive pytest_plugins list and write it back into `context`: read the loader section of the plugin config, build `PackageLoader`/`ModuleLoader` from its packages/modules, prepend the explicit `loaders`, drive each loader to append discovered dotted names, then deduplicate the list.
    4. Must complete synchronously on construction so pytest_plugins is populated before import finishes.

    Use `pluginator` for the @define.plugin contract and the BasePlugin.__init__ it overrides, the define.option descriptor, and the option resolution chain (plugin_config_key → env → CLI → default_from → required/nullable).
    Use `conventions` for type hints, relative imports, and kw_only usage where applicable.
    Use `registration` and `discovery` for the loader-driven registration step.
  properties:
    "base_url -> str": |
      Service base URL as a Jinja2 template string. The option value resolves following `pluginator`: plugin config → env BASE_URL → CLI --base-url → required (no default). Additionally, when the user actually typed --base-url on the CLI, configure applies that value with top precedence over the plugin config and env before rendering (the pluginator chain alone would let config/env win; the CLI is authoritative for base_url). Only the interpretation of the value is specific to this cell: it is rendered once in configure against the full environment plus the passed CLI options (see configure), and the rendered value is stored back onto this option. Rendering uses a single engine, Jinja2 (a required dependency), with StrictUndefined (an unknown variable raises) and a custom match_re test; a plain URL without Jinja placeholders renders to itself; every whitespace run in the rendered value is removed (a URL never legitimately contains literal whitespace), so a multi-line template yields one clean URL.
      The option stays required and rendered in configure exactly as before even when the
      sandbox document is present: a test that binds its api to the sandbox overrides the
      address at bind time (see `api_usage`); every other test uses the rendered value.
    "headers -> dict[str, str]": |
      Resolved default headers dict. Default {} when nothing is configured. Sourced from the headers plugin config key.
    "timeout -> float | None": |
      Resolved network timeout. Default None. Resolution: plugin config → env API_TIMEOUT → CLI --api-timeout → None.
    "retries -> int": |
      Resolved flaky rerun count for the test run. Default 0 (no reruns). Resolution: plugin config (PluginConfigKeys.RETRIES) → CLI --retries → default_from the constructor default → int() (0). Orthogonal to the api fixture: when positive, pytest_collection_modifyitems stamps unmarked items with pytest.mark.flaky(max_runs=retries).
    "assert_timeout -> int | None": |
      Resolved baseline assert-polling timeout. Default None (no polling). Resolution: plugin config (PluginConfigKeys.ASSERT_TIMEOUT) → CLI --api-assert-timeout → default_from the constructor default → None. Forwarded into Api → AssertConfig to drive matchcrest's retry loop.
    "assert_delay -> float | None": |
      Resolved seconds between assert-polling attempts. Default None. Resolution: plugin config (PluginConfigKeys.ASSERT_DELAY) → CLI --api-assert-delay → default_from the constructor default → None. Forwarded into Api → AssertConfig.
    "assert_field_class -> str | None": |
      Resolved dotted module:Class path of a custom AssertField subclass. Default None (built-in AssertField). Sourced from the assert_field_class plugin config key. Forwarded into Api → AssertConfig; resolved by Expect.__call__ via load_assert_class.
    "assert_response_class -> str | None": |
      Resolved dotted module:Class path of a custom Expect subclass. Default None (built-in Expect). Sourced from the assert_response_class plugin config key. Forwarded into Api → AssertConfig; resolved by ResponseWrapper.expect via load_assert_class.
  methods:
    "configure()": |
      Pluginator lifecycle callback (NOT a pytest hookimpl). Renders the base_url template once, at pytest configphase, against the full environment plus the CLI options the user actually passed, with Jinja2, and stores the rendered value back onto the base_url option for the api fixture to consume. Pluginator auto-discovers a no-arg configure method and calls it from pytest_configure after config init and install, when the pytest config and the resolved base_url are both available (see `pluginator`).

      Algorithm:
      1. Collect the CLI options the user actually typed: take the option tokens from the CLI, normalize their names, take their resolved values, and drop None. The full resolved option namespace is intentionally NOT used — it carries internal/plugin options that must not leak into the template (see `pluginator` for the config-time CLI sources).
      2. When the user typed the base_url CLI flag (--base-url), store its resolved value onto the base_url option — a typed --base-url overrides the plugin config and BASE_URL resolution (the pluginator chain resolves the config before the CLI; the CLI is authoritative for base_url).
      3. Build the rendering context as a plain dict initialized with the full os.environ and merge in the collected CLI options.
      4. Render the template once via `render_base_url` (Jinja2; StrictUndefined; the match_re test registered) against the rendering context, and store the result back onto the base_url option. `render_base_url` also removes every whitespace run from the rendered URL, so a multi-line base_url template renders to a single clean URL. A plain base_url without placeholders renders to itself.

      Requirements:
      - Runs once at pytest configphase, before any test or fixture invocation.
      - The pytest config must be initialized (pluginator calls this after config init).

      Constraints:
      - Rendering uses Jinja2 only (a required dependency declared in pyproject.toml).
      - Jinja2 uses StrictUndefined: an unknown variable raises (URLs must not be silently truncated).
      - Rendering is performed by the `render_base_url` routine, which also normalizes the URL (removes every whitespace run).

      Use `pluginator` for the configure() lifecycle discovery and the pytest_config contract.
      Use `jinja2` for the Jinja2 engine configuration, StrictUndefined, and the match_re test.
      Use `conventions` for type hints and relative imports.
    "api() -> api: Iterator[Api]": |
      Function-scope generator fixture yielding an `Api` built from the resolved plugin options and closing it after the test.

      api: the `Api` client consumed by generated endpoint fixtures.

      Algorithm:
      1. Construct `Api` from the resolved options — the rendered self.base_url plus headers,
      timeout, and the assert options (minimal profile; no auth, no cookies).
      2. Yield the `Api` to the test.
      3. After the test, call api.close() to close the Api (delegates to the underlying resq.Session's public close()).

      Constraints:
      - Do not configure auth or cookies — minimal profile.
      - Consume the base_url option as rendered by configure (no rendering inside the fixture).
      - No fixture-closure sniffing — the fixture never resolves the sandbox; binding an
      address (the running sandbox) is explicit in the test body (see `api_usage`).

      Use `pluginator` for the fixture-as-method convention.
      Use `conventions` for type hints.
    "pytest_collection_modifyitems(items: list[pytest.Item])": |
      pytest hook (tryfirst). When retries resolves to a positive int, stamp every collected item without an existing flaky marker with pytest.mark.flaky(max_runs=retries); items already carrying a flaky marker are left untouched to avoid double-marking. The flaky package is NOT disabled here — the marker is the contract surface and takes effect when flaky is installed in the consumer suite.

      Algorithm:
      1. Skip unless self.retries is truthy and greater than 0.
      2. For each item without an existing flaky marker, stamp it with pytest.mark.flaky(max_runs=retries).

      Use `pluginator` for the hook discovery via pluginmanager registration in pytest_configure.
      Use `conventions` for type hints.

"install(...kwargs: object)":
  location: __init__.py
  annotations: |
    Install the plugin into pytest at import time, then synchronously register discovered generated fixtures.

    `kwargs`: context (defaulted via call_context()) and loaders (defaulted to the api package discovery) are forwarded to `ApiPlugin`; ApiPlugin.__init__ then registers discovered plugins, populating the pytest_plugins list.

    Algorithm:
    1. Default context to call_context(), which resolves to the caller module's globals through the one-level wrapper — so calling `install` from conftest.py targets the conftest namespace (where pytest picks up the hooks and pytest_plugins).
    2. Default loaders to [PackageLoader('api', required=False)] when omitted (auto-discovery of the api/ tree); an explicit loaders overrides it, loaders=[] disables it.
    3. Gate the `ApiPlugin` on the presence of the api document (the file check of the
       plugins-cell default path): with the document, construct an `ApiPlugin` instance
       (context and loaders are forwarded; ApiPlugin.__init__ registers discovered plugins
       synchronously, populating the pytest_plugins list); without the document nothing
       api-related is registered — the required base_url never fails a web-only or
       sandbox-only run — and a test requesting the api fixture gets pytest's standard
       "fixture 'api' not found". BASE_URL (the env var) is a value source of the base_url
       option, never an activation source of the plugin.
    4. Gate the `SandboxPlugin` on the presence of the sandbox document (the file check of the
    plugin-cell default path): with the document, normalize the container-host address first
    (`normalize_container_host` — defaults the TESTCONTAINERS_HOST_OVERRIDE env so a plain
    host run and a container run share one address space; see `testcontainers`),
    then append a `SandboxPlugin` instance to the same positional plugin list — the plugin reads
    and validates its config itself in its configure step; without the document nothing
    sandbox-related is registered (the address normalization included), and a test requesting
    the sandbox fixture gets pytest's standard "fixture 'sandbox' not found".
    5. Gate the `WebPlugin` on the presence of the web config document (the file check of the
       plugins-cell default path — the platform tool-config path named by the FILENAME member of
       the cell's own `WebConfigKeys`): with the document,
       append a `WebPlugin` instance to the same positional plugin list — the plugin reads and
       validates its config itself in its configure step; without the document nothing web-related
       is registered, and a test requesting the web fixture gets pytest's standard "fixture
       'web' not found".
    6. Call install_pytest_plugins with every gated plugin to inject pytest_addoption/
       pytest_configure/pytest_collection_finish into that namespace (see `pluginator`).

    Requirements:
    - `install` must be the one-level wrapper around call_context(); calling call_context() directly would resolve the wrong stack frame.
    - The loader step must run synchronously so pytest_plugins is populated before import finishes.
    - The address normalization runs only with the document present, defaults the env (never
      overrides an explicit value), and its failure is a no-op — a failed detection keeps the
      mapped-localhost behavior.
    - The same installation call enables every plugin whose activation document is present — the
    `ApiPlugin` (the api document), the `SandboxPlugin` (the sandbox document), and the
    `WebPlugin` (the web document); no new enablement surface exists.
    - The web gate performs no validation — the document is only checked for presence; validation
    belongs to the web plugin's configure step (an invalid document fails at configphase).

    Use `pluginator` for install_pytest_plugins and call_context.
    Use `registration` for the loader-driven registration step.
    Use `testcontainers` for the container-host override seam of the address normalization.
    Use `pybuggy-web` from Imports for the consumer patterns of the web test kind.

"SandboxPlugin()":
  location: sandbox_plugin.py
  annotations: |
    Pluginator plugin exposing the product-owned sandbox fixtures. The sandbox document
    ``.goga/tools/pybuggy/sandbox.yml`` is the plugin's config: pluginator reads it into
    ``plugin_config`` (see `pluginator`), and configure validates it into the activation —
    an invalid document fails the run at configphase, before any collection or container.
    Zero options; the installation entry constructs the plugin only when the document is
    present (see `install`).

    Use `pluginator` for the plugin-config reading, the zero-option plugin shape, the
    configure() lifecycle callback, and fixtures as plugin-class methods.
    Use `conventions` for docstrings and relative imports.

  properties:
    "activation -> SandboxConfig | None": |
      The validated `SandboxConfig` — the document the configure step validated; None
      before configure ran. The session fixture starts the sandbox from it.
  methods:
    "configure()": |
      Register the ``pybuggy_services`` marker and validate the document into the activation.
      Runs at pytest configphase, before collection: the marker registration keeps applying
      the marker in test modules warning-free; the validation fails fast — an unparsable
      document (a parse error of the config reader) wraps into a ValueError naming the
      document location; an empty document fails with the explicit "must carry the instance
      entry" error; an invalid document surfaces the validation error of
      `validate_sandbox_config`. On success sets ``activation`` and logs the armed state.
    "sandbox() -> sandbox: Iterator[Sandbox]": |
      Session-scope generator fixture: build `Sandbox` from the activation, start it (lazy —
      the first test that requests the sandbox starts the containers), yield it for the whole
      session, stop it on finalization. The same object on every use within a session.

      `sandbox`: the running session sandbox.
    "_sandbox_test_batch(request: pytest.FixtureRequest)": |
      Autouse function-scope fixture giving the next sandbox test a fresh baseline and its
      marked presets. Inert for tests that do not use the sandbox: the fixturenames guard
      keeps the globally-autouse registration harmless for the rest of the suite. For a
      sandbox test: resolve the session sandbox through ``request.getfixturevalue("sandbox")``,
      reset every engine to the baseline (``Sandbox.clear``), create the fresh batch
      (``Sandbox.new_test_batch``), then enqueue every ``pybuggy_services`` marker's
      ``presets`` kwarg (``Sandbox.enqueue_presets``) in the order the item carries the
      markers.

      `request`: the fixture request of the test being set up.

"WebPlugin()":
  location: web_plugin.py
  annotations: |
    Pluginator plugin of the web test kind: the web fixture, the fixed web.yml key set
    (`WebConfigKeys` — declared in this module), the strict/interactive/endpoint options, Allure
    step reporting, and the prettyplay event log formatter. The web config document web.yml is the
    plugin's config: pluginator reads it into plugin_config, and configure validates it into the
    activation — an invalid document fails the run at configphase, before any collection or browser.

    Constructed only by the installation gate of this cell's installation entry (`install` — the
    document presence check); zero constructor arguments; no work at construction — the activation
    and the formatter land in configure.

    Algorithm:
    1. Declare the strict and interactive options — the plugin config keys strict and interactive of
       the cell's `WebConfigKeys`, CLI flags --strict-mode and --interactive (store_true) — and the browser
       endpoint option — a nullable value option with the CLI flag --browser-endpoint (store) and no
       plugin-config key (the nested browser.endpoint document key never resolves through the
       pluginator chain; a typed flag reaches the document through the configure-time injection).
    2. Nothing else at construction.

    Use `pluginator` for the @define.plugin contract, define.option, fixtures as plugin-class methods,
    and the configure() lifecycle.
    Use `prettyplay` for the runtime contract consumed by the fixture.
    Use `allure-pytest` for the guarded activation of the reporting hooks.
    Use `conventions` for docstrings, type hints, and relative imports.
  properties:
    "strict -> bool": |
      Resolved strict mode: the web config strict key → the CLI --strict-mode flag (a typed flag
      forces true — the CLI authority applied in configure, the pattern of --base-url) → the default
      False. Strict runs are replay-only: zero LLM calls, a cache miss fails the test.
    "interactive -> bool": |
      Resolved interactive healing mode: the web config interactive key → the CLI --interactive flag
      (the same CLI-authority form) → the default False.
    "browser_endpoint -> str | None": |
      The typed --browser-endpoint CLI value — a remote Playwright endpoint (ws/wss or http/https,
      the CDP family). No plugin-config key and no env source: the document value of
      browser.endpoint flows through the document itself, and a typed flag overrides it in
      configure before validation (see configure). None when the flag is not typed — the option
      never carries the document value.
    "activation -> PrettyConfig | None": |
      The validated web config — the library prettyplay model (see `prettyplay`), the document the
      configure step validated; None before configure ran. The fixture builds the runtime from it.
  methods:
    "configure()": |
      Validate the document into the activation, apply the CLI authority to the options and the
      typed endpoint to the document, scrub the library's env layer, and install the prettyplay
      event log formatter.

      Algorithm:
      1. Apply CLI authority: when the user typed --strict-mode or --interactive, store True onto
         the option — a typed flag overrides the config-file and env resolution (see `pluginator` for
         the config-time CLI sources).
      2. Read plugin_config under a parse guard: an unparsable document (the
         config reader's parse error) surfaces as the clean error naming the web config document —
         the pattern of the
         sandbox plugin; never a raw reader traceback. A non-mapping parse fails with the explicit
         mapping error naming the document.
      3. Apply the endpoint authority: when the user typed --browser-endpoint, inject the typed
         value into the browser section of the document copy (creating the section when the document
         carries none or a null one) — the typed value applies verbatim, an empty string included
         (it forces the local launch over a document endpoint).
      4. Reject an empty document copy — the document must carry at least the surveyed minimum (the
         provider and the browser name) — with the explicit empty-document error naming the document.
      5. Validate the composed document copy into the activation via PrettyConfig.model_validate —
         the library model with the library validators, so a structural or a semantic violation (an
         off-matrix browser name, an invalid endpoint — a typed one included) fails with its clean
         error at configphase (the pydantic detail chained).
      6. Scrub the PRETTYPLAY_* environment variables of the process (one INFO log naming them) —
         web.yml is the single configuration source; the library's env layer never overrides it.
      7. Install the prettyplay event log formatter via `install_event_log_formatter`.

      Requirements:
      - Runs once at pytest configphase, before any collection and before any fixture invocation.
      - An unparsable document fails at configphase with the clean error naming web.yml — never a
        raw reader traceback.
      - A non-mapping or empty document fails with its explicit error naming web.yml — the same gate
        order the former web-cell validation routine carried.
      - A typed --browser-endpoint passes the same document gate: an invalid endpoint fails at
        configphase with the document error, never at a browser start.
      - The endpoint injection works on the document copy — the document object the reader supplied
        is never mutated.
      - The env scrub is permanent for the run and race-free — configphase is single-threaded; the
        variables exist only for the library's env layer.
      - The formatter is installed unconditionally with the plugin active — it is not allure-gated.

      Use `pluginator` for the configure lifecycle and the config-time CLI sources.
      Use `prettyplay` for the env layer the scrub neutralizes.
    "web(request: pytest.FixtureRequest) -> web: Iterator[PrettyPlay]": |
      Function-scope generator fixture: build the per-test prettyplay runtime and close it after the
      test.

      `request`: the fixture request of the test being set up.
      web: the opened prettyplay runtime the test consumes — step, expect, group, run_on_page (see
      `prettyplay`).

      Algorithm:
      1. Compute the cache address: the root is the activation cache_root when set, otherwise the
         absolute directory of the requesting test's own module file (from the item — never the
         cwd); the per-test path is `web_cache_path` of request.node; the cache key is the item's name
         (parametrize-aware).
      2. Build the explicit prettyplay config via `build_pretty_config` from the activation and the
         resolved strict and interactive options.
      3. Construct the prettyplay runtime with the cache key, the path, and the config, with
         cleanup disabled (the persistence mode of the committed-cache workflow: every cached
         file of the test's key is kept — a run that fails mid-test must not destroy the cached
         steps it never reached, so the next strict replay stays green; a stale step is removed
         by deleting its file).
      4. When allure is importable (see `allure-pytest`), attach `AllureStepHooks` to the runtime.
      5. Open the runtime (the context manager), yield it, close it on finalization.

      Requirements:
      - A --strict-mode run makes zero LLM calls — a cache miss fails the test.
      - The step cache lands next to the test file and is committable; the addressing never depends
        on the cwd — tests running outside the project root resolve their paths from the item
        itself.

      Constraints:
      - No base_url templating and no sandbox binding — web steps carry full URLs.
      - Do not construct `AllureStepHooks` when the guarded import reports allure absent.

      Use `pluginator` for the fixture-as-method convention.
      Use `prettyplay` for the runtime construction and the cache addressing contract.
      Use `conventions` for type hints.

"web_cache_path(item: pytest.Item) -> path: str":
  location: web_plugin.py
  annotations: |
    Compute the per-test step-cache path from the requesting test item.

    `item`: the pytest item of the requesting test.
    `path`: the posix-relative subdirectory inside the cache root — <module_stem>/<ClassName>/<test_name>
      with a class, <module_stem>/<test_name> without.

    Algorithm:
    1. Take the module stem from the item's own module file path.
    2. Take the original name — originalname when present, the node name otherwise: the path is
       parametrize-agnostic while the cache key stays parametrize-aware.
    3. Insert the class-name segment when the item carries a class.
    4. Join the segments with forward slashes.

    Requirements:
    - Derived from the item's own file — never from the cwd.
    - Pure — no filesystem access.

    Use `prettyplay` for the cache addressing contract (the cache key and the path form the address).
    Use `conventions` for type hints.

